  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Implementation**\n\nOkay, I\'m now zeroing in on the core of this task. It involves integrating text-to-speech functionality directly into this Gemini-driven browser. The browser\'s native API, SpeechSynthesis, is the key here. The primary function will be reading the rendered page content aloud. I need to make sure the header in \"Flash-Lite Browser\" updates as well. I believe this can be done without too much work, as there are existing tools.\n\n\n**Updating the Branding**\n\nI\'ve just finished updating all the branding strings. I went through `OuterFrame.tsx`, `NewTab.tsx`, `metadata.json`, `index.html`, and `index.css` to change all instances of \"Flash-Lite Browser\" to \"AgentSamRemix Browser\" and updated the related descriptions, as well. Now, I\'m shifting focus to the design of the text-to-speech feature, considering the optimal user interface for controlling it.\n\n\n**Designing the Controls**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the user interface for the text-to-speech feature. I\'ve been examining `AddressBar.tsx` and the browser UI. I am thinking that a dedicated \"Read Aloud\" button directly in the address bar (perhaps next to the omnibar, within the action buttons, or as a new item in the 3-dots menu) would be incredibly accessible. The goal is to provide intuitive playback controls (play/pause/stop) with a floating bar or indicator within the UI to signal state at any time.\n\n\n**Refining the Speech Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the finer details. I am considering edge cases, specifically navigation or tab changes during speech playback, ensuring smooth transitions by stopping the reading gracefully. I need to make sure the \"Read Aloud\" button\'s behavior adapts intuitively to the current state, handling pause/resume/stop actions effectively, and disabling the feature on the new tab screen. I\'m also exploring how to extract plain text from the rendered HTML in `App.tsx` efficiently, for input into the text-to-speech engine, especially focusing on methods using `DOMParser`.\n\n\n**Enhancing Text Extraction**\n\nI\'m now refining the text extraction logic for text-to-speech. I\'ve realized that just grabbing innerText isn\'t sufficient for natural reading. I am adding spacing between block-level elements like headings, paragraphs, and list items. I\'ll make sure to clean up the HTML, then use `DOMParser`. I also need to provide support for reading only selected text or the whole page text. I am also checking the `SpeechSynthesis` API details.\n\n\n**Implementing the TTS API**\n\nI am now focusing on integrating the `SpeechSynthesis` API. I\'m taking notes on its methods such as `speak()`, `cancel()`, and the use of `SpeechSynthesisUtterance`. I\'m paying close attention to event listeners (`onstart`, `onend`, etc.) for managing the UI state, as well as `getVoices()` to offer voice selection. I also need to address potential browser quirks with long utterances using chunking.\n\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 726562768
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
