steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787970319
      nanos: 789845785
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "\'spinning up new enviorment\' - note it. the thoughts then terminal commands. note it. click to expan, note it. 2- network config, whats this files.pythonhosted.org and note the ui ux , clean. simple. well put toghether\n\nYes \342\200\224 for experimenting with the AgentSam AntiGravity idea right now, Google\342\200\231s managed Antigravity environment is unusually cheap, because Google currently says the remote sandbox CPU/memory/execution itself is not billed during the preview. You pay for the Gemini model tokens and billable tools the agent consumes.\n\nYour existing AgentSam sandbox lane is Cloudflare Containers using the basic instance type. Cloudflare\342\200\231s current basic allocation is \302\274 vCPU, 1 GiB RAM, 4 GB disk. After the Workers Paid monthly inclusions, that works out to roughly $0.028/hour at continuous full CPU utilization, or about $0.010/hour if it stays alive without meaningful CPU use, before the associated Worker/DO usage. Your $5 Workers Paid plan includes 25 GiB-hours memory, 375 vCPU-minutes, and 200 GB-hours disk, so roughly the first 25 fully-loaded basic-container hours fit inside those compute inclusions, assuming other container usage hasn\'t already consumed them.\n\nSo the comparison is more interesting than \342\200\234Google VM vs Cloudflare VM\342\200\235:\n\n\tAgentSam Cloudflare sandbox\tGoogle Antigravity\nLinux environment\tYes\tYes\nEnvironment provisioning\tYou own it\tGoogle handles it\nCompute charge\tTiny, usage-based\t$0 during preview\nAgent harness\tAgentSam owns it\tIncluded\nShell/filesystem loop\tAgentSam owns it\tIncluded\nPersistence\tYour implementation\tEnvironment IDs / persistent files\nNetwork policy\tYour implementation\tBuilt-in allowlist\nExecution-stream semantics\tYour implementation\tIncluded\nModel\tWhatever AgentSam routes\tGemini 3.7 Flash by default\nMain cost driver\tModel + some container compute\tModel/tool tokens\n\nThe key point is that Cloudflare compute was probably never the expensive part of AgentSam. It\'s pennies. The expensive thing is the intelligent loop repeatedly reading context, reasoning, calling tools and feeding results back into the model.\n\nGoogle\'s current Antigravity docs actually give useful estimates. They report typical system-design runs around $0.25\342\200\223$0.80, data-analysis runs around $0.70\342\200\223$3.25, and say particularly complex multi-tool interactions can accumulate 3\342\200\2235 million tokens and reach roughly $5.\n\nGemini 3.7 Flash is currently promotional-priced through December 31, 2026 at $0.75/M input tokens and $3.75/M output tokens, including thinking tokens. There is also a free tier.\n\nAnd your screenshot is revealing: Antigravity had already reached 51,711 tokens while happily continuing through terminal operations. Even in the worst simplistic case of treating all 51.7k as output-priced tokens, that\'s only about 19\302\242 at the current 3.7 Flash rate; actual billing depends on the input/output/cache/tool mix. More importantly, Google is explicitly building around the kind of 10-, 20-, 50-tool-call mission you\'ve wanted instead of treating multiple tool executions like an exceptional case.\n\nWhat I\'d do with this\n\nI would not replace your Cloudflare Container authority with Google Antigravity.\n\nI would, however, use Antigravity aggressively as an experimental execution backend for AgentSam while the environment compute is free.\n\nYour execution abstraction can become something like:\n\nAgentSam Mission\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\226\274\nExecutionEnvironment\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 local\n      \342\224\202     \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Local Mac / localpty\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 sandbox\n      \342\224\202     \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Cloudflare Container\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 remote\n      \342\224\202     \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 GCP VM\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 managed\n            \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Google Antigravity\n\nThen instead of rebuilding Google\'s whole managed-agent machinery before knowing what parts matter, run real AgentSam-grade missions through it and measure it.\n\nSpecifically, the thing Google just generated for you is already a great benchmark. Give Antigravity a real repository snapshot and ask it to do something like:\n\nMISSION\nAudit this repository for competing identity authorities.\n\nDo not modify anything.\n\nInspect as much of the repository as necessary.\nUse shell/search/filesystem tools freely.\nMaintain a structured execution stream.\nIdentify concrete ownership overlap with file evidence.\nGenerate an SVG architecture map.\nProduce a proposed consolidation sequence.\nStop after producing the report.\n\nThen capture:\n\nmission duration\ntotal tokens\ntool calls\nterminal calls\nfiles inspected\nsearches\nfailures/retries\ncost\nquality of result\namount of human intervention\n\nRun essentially the same mission through AgentSam + Cloudflare Sandbox.\n\nThat\'s the comparison that matters. Not:\n\nWhich container costs less?\n\nBut:\n\nHow many dollars and how much infrastructure does it take to get one successful autonomous engineering mission?\n\nCloudflare could cost 3\302\242 of compute and still be the more expensive system if AgentSam burns $4 of model tokens because its orchestration keeps rereading context or getting stuck. Antigravity could cost $1.20 in Gemini tokens with free compute and finish in one clean loop.\n\nOr the reverse could happen: your AgentSam router could use cheaper models intelligently and perform the same mission for 30\302\242 while retaining complete infrastructure ownership.\n\nThat\'s a benchmark I\'d actually trust.\n\nOne warning: Google\'s free environment compute is explicitly a preview condition, not something I\'d bake into AgentSam\'s long-term economics. Cloudflare\'s economics, by contrast, are already explicit usage pricing. So AntiGravity is a fantastic managed lane / reference implementation / competitive benchmark right now, while Cloudflare remains the much more predictable substrate you control.\n\nAnd the fact that Google lets you download the resulting environment snapshot makes this experiment even nicer: you can let it build AgentSam AntiGravity in its sandbox, inspect the completed environment, and pull the result out rather than treating that remote environment as the permanent home of the app.\n\nhow"
    items {
      text: "\'spinning up new enviorment\' - note it. the thoughts then terminal commands. note it. click to expan, note it. 2- network config, whats this files.pythonhosted.org and note the ui ux , clean. simple. well put toghether\n\nYes \342\200\224 for experimenting with the AgentSam AntiGravity idea right now, Google\342\200\231s managed Antigravity environment is unusually cheap, because Google currently says the remote sandbox CPU/memory/execution itself is not billed during the preview. You pay for the Gemini model tokens and billable tools the agent consumes.\n\nYour existing AgentSam sandbox lane is Cloudflare Containers using the basic instance type. Cloudflare\342\200\231s current basic allocation is \302\274 vCPU, 1 GiB RAM, 4 GB disk. After the Workers Paid monthly inclusions, that works out to roughly $0.028/hour at continuous full CPU utilization, or about $0.010/hour if it stays alive without meaningful CPU use, before the associated Worker/DO usage. Your $5 Workers Paid plan includes 25 GiB-hours memory, 375 vCPU-minutes, and 200 GB-hours disk, so roughly the first 25 fully-loaded basic-container hours fit inside those compute inclusions, assuming other container usage hasn\'t already consumed them.\n\nSo the comparison is more interesting than \342\200\234Google VM vs Cloudflare VM\342\200\235:\n\n\tAgentSam Cloudflare sandbox\tGoogle Antigravity\nLinux environment\tYes\tYes\nEnvironment provisioning\tYou own it\tGoogle handles it\nCompute charge\tTiny, usage-based\t$0 during preview\nAgent harness\tAgentSam owns it\tIncluded\nShell/filesystem loop\tAgentSam owns it\tIncluded\nPersistence\tYour implementation\tEnvironment IDs / persistent files\nNetwork policy\tYour implementation\tBuilt-in allowlist\nExecution-stream semantics\tYour implementation\tIncluded\nModel\tWhatever AgentSam routes\tGemini 3.7 Flash by default\nMain cost driver\tModel + some container compute\tModel/tool tokens\n\nThe key point is that Cloudflare compute was probably never the expensive part of AgentSam. It\'s pennies. The expensive thing is the intelligent loop repeatedly reading context, reasoning, calling tools and feeding results back into the model.\n\nGoogle\'s current Antigravity docs actually give useful estimates. They report typical system-design runs around $0.25\342\200\223$0.80, data-analysis runs around $0.70\342\200\223$3.25, and say particularly complex multi-tool interactions can accumulate 3\342\200\2235 million tokens and reach roughly $5.\n\nGemini 3.7 Flash is currently promotional-priced through December 31, 2026 at $0.75/M input tokens and $3.75/M output tokens, including thinking tokens. There is also a free tier.\n\nAnd your screenshot is revealing: Antigravity had already reached 51,711 tokens while happily continuing through terminal operations. Even in the worst simplistic case of treating all 51.7k as output-priced tokens, that\'s only about 19\302\242 at the current 3.7 Flash rate; actual billing depends on the input/output/cache/tool mix. More importantly, Google is explicitly building around the kind of 10-, 20-, 50-tool-call mission you\'ve wanted instead of treating multiple tool executions like an exceptional case.\n\nWhat I\'d do with this\n\nI would not replace your Cloudflare Container authority with Google Antigravity.\n\nI would, however, use Antigravity aggressively as an experimental execution backend for AgentSam while the environment compute is free.\n\nYour execution abstraction can become something like:\n\nAgentSam Mission\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\226\274\nExecutionEnvironment\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 local\n      \342\224\202     \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Local Mac / localpty\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 sandbox\n      \342\224\202     \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Cloudflare Container\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 remote\n      \342\224\202     \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 GCP VM\n      \342\224\202\n      \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 managed\n            \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Google Antigravity\n\nThen instead of rebuilding Google\'s whole managed-agent machinery before knowing what parts matter, run real AgentSam-grade missions through it and measure it.\n\nSpecifically, the thing Google just generated for you is already a great benchmark. Give Antigravity a real repository snapshot and ask it to do something like:\n\nMISSION\nAudit this repository for competing identity authorities.\n\nDo not modify anything.\n\nInspect as much of the repository as necessary.\nUse shell/search/filesystem tools freely.\nMaintain a structured execution stream.\nIdentify concrete ownership overlap with file evidence.\nGenerate an SVG architecture map.\nProduce a proposed consolidation sequence.\nStop after producing the report.\n\nThen capture:\n\nmission duration\ntotal tokens\ntool calls\nterminal calls\nfiles inspected\nsearches\nfailures/retries\ncost\nquality of result\namount of human intervention\n\nRun essentially the same mission through AgentSam + Cloudflare Sandbox.\n\nThat\'s the comparison that matters. Not:\n\nWhich container costs less?\n\nBut:\n\nHow many dollars and how much infrastructure does it take to get one successful autonomous engineering mission?\n\nCloudflare could cost 3\302\242 of compute and still be the more expensive system if AgentSam burns $4 of model tokens because its orchestration keeps rereading context or getting stuck. Antigravity could cost $1.20 in Gemini tokens with free compute and finish in one clean loop.\n\nOr the reverse could happen: your AgentSam router could use cheaper models intelligently and perform the same mission for 30\302\242 while retaining complete infrastructure ownership.\n\nThat\'s a benchmark I\'d actually trust.\n\nOne warning: Google\'s free environment compute is explicitly a preview condition, not something I\'d bake into AgentSam\'s long-term economics. Cloudflare\'s economics, by contrast, are already explicit usage pricing. So AntiGravity is a fantastic managed lane / reference implementation / competitive benchmark right now, while Cloudflare remains the much more predictable substrate you control.\n\nAnd the fact that Google lets you download the resulting environment snapshot makes this experiment even nicer: you can let it build AgentSam AntiGravity in its sandbox, inspect the completed environment, and pull the result out rather than treating that remote environment as the permanent home of the app.\n\nhow"
